## Quick‑start Guide: Writing Unit Tests with **JUnit 5** (JUnit Jupiter)

JUnit 5 is the next‑generation testing framework for Java.  
It is split into three main parts:

| Component | What it does |
|-----------|--------------|
| **JUnit Platform** | The foundation that hosts and discovers tests. |
| **JUnit Jupiter** | The **API** (annotations, assertions, extensions) and the **engine** that runs Jupiter tests. |
| **JUnit Vintage** | Optional engine that lets you run old JUnit 4 or 3 tests on the same platform. |

> **Tip** – For a *plain Java* project you’ll only need the **Jupiter** dependency.  
> If you’re on a build tool, JUnit 5 is shipped as a *set of artifacts*; add the one you need.

---

## 1. Add the JUnit 5 dependency

### Maven
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
    <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
    <version>5.11.3</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

### Gradle (Groovy DSL)
```groovy
testImplementation('org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.11.3')
```

### Gradle (Kotlin DSL)
```kotlin
testImplementation("org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.11.3")
```

> **IDE integration** – Most IDEs (IntelliJ, Eclipse, VS Code + Java extension) will automatically pick up the dependency and offer “Run tests” actions.

---

## 2. Create a simple test class

Assume you have a small `Calculator` class you want to test.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/Calculator.java
package com.example;

public class Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b)          { return a + b; }
    public int subtract(int a, int b)     { return a - b; }
    public int multiply(int a, int b)     { return a * b; }
    public int divide(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) throw new ArithmeticException("division by zero");
        return a / b;
    }
}
```

### Test class skeleton

```java
// src/test/java/com/example/CalculatorTest.java
package com.example;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS) // optional – allows non‑static @BeforeAll
@TestMethodOrder(MethodOrderer.OrderAnnotation.class) // optional – gives explicit test order
class CalculatorTest {

    private Calculator calculator;

    @BeforeAll
    void initAll() {
        System.out.println("Initializing all tests…");
    }

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        calculator = new Calculator();
    }

    @AfterEach
    void tearDown() {
        calculator = null;
    }

    @AfterAll
    void tearDownAll() {
        System.out.println("All tests finished.");
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Addition should return correct sum")
    @Order(1)
    void testAddition() {
        assertEquals(5, calculator.add(2, 3));
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Subtracting should return difference")
    @Order(2)
    void testSubtract() {
        assertEquals(-1, calculator.subtract(2, 3));
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Division by zero should throw ArithmeticException")
    void testDivideByZero() {
        assertThrows(ArithmeticException.class,
                     () -> calculator.divide(10, 0),
                     "division by zero");
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Multiplication should return product")
    void testMultiply() {
        assertAll(
            () -> assertEquals(12, calculator.multiply(3, 4)),
            () -> assertEquals(0,  calculator.multiply(0, 5))
        );
    }

    @ParameterizedTest(name = "{index} => a={0}, b={1}, expected={2}")
    @CsvSource({
        "2, 3, 5",
        "-1, 5, 4",
        "0, 0, 0"
    })
    void testAdditionParameterized(int a, int b, int expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, calculator.add(a, b));
    }
}
```

### What you’re seeing

| Annotation | Purpose | Example |
|------------|---------|---------|
| `@Test` | Marks a method as a test case. | `void testAddition() { … }` |
| `@DisplayName` | A human‑readable name that shows up in IDE reports. | `@DisplayName("Addition should return correct sum")` |
| `@Order` | Explicit test execution order (only if you really need it). | `@Order(1)` |
| `@BeforeAll` / `@AfterAll` | Run once before/after all tests. Must be `static` *unless* the test class is `@TestInstance(PER_CLASS)`. | `void initAll()` |
| `@BeforeEach` / `@AfterEach` | Run before/after each test method. | `void setUp()` |
| `@ParameterizedTest` | Parameterised test: the same logic executed with many data sets. | `@CsvSource` |
| `@Disabled` | Skip a test (or entire class). | `@Disabled("WIP")` |
| `@Tag` | Logical grouping for selective runs (e.g., `@Tag("fast")`). | `@Tag("fast")` |
| `@ExtendWith` | Plug in extensions (e.g., Mockito, Spring Test). | `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)` |
| `@Nested` | Logical grouping of related tests inside a top‑level class. | `class DivisionTests { … }` |

---

## 3. Assertions – the backbone of tests

| Assertion | When to use |
|-----------|-------------|
| `assertEquals(expected, actual)` | Values must match. |
| `assertTrue(condition)` / `assertFalse(condition)` | Boolean conditions. |
| `assertNull(value)` / `assertNotNull(value)` | Nullity checks. |
| `assertSame(expected, actual)` / `assertNotSame(...)` | Identity checks (`==`). |
| `assertArrayEquals(expected, actual)` | Array comparison. |
| `assertThrows(expectedType, executable)` | Ensure an exception is thrown. |
| `assertDoesNotThrow(executable)` | Ensure no exception occurs. |
| `assertAll(Executable... executables)` | Group multiple assertions; all failures are reported. |
| `assertTimeout(duration, executable)` | Check execution time stays under a limit. |
| `assertTimeoutPreemptively(duration, executable)` | Force test to fail if timeout is reached (pre‑emptive). |

> **Static imports** are handy:  
> `import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;`

---

## 4. Advanced: Parameterised Tests

JUnit 5 supports many data sources:

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@ValueSource(strings = {"Hello", "World"})
void testNonEmpty(String input) {
    assertNotNull(input);
}
```

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@CsvSource({
  "1, 2, 3",
  "-1, 5, 4"
})
void testAdditionCsv(int a, int b, int expected) {
    assertEquals(expected, calculator.add(a, b));
}
```

```java
@ParameterizedTest
@MethodSource("stringProvider")
void testWithMethodSource(String argument) {
    assertNotNull(argument);
}

static Stream<String> stringProvider() {
    return Stream.of("foo", "bar");
}
```

---

## 5. Extensions & Extensions API

JUnit 5’s **Extension Model** lets you plug in behaviour without modifying test code.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class CalculatorTestWithMock {
    @Mock SomeDependency mockDep;
    // …
}
```

Custom extensions can do anything from injecting parameters to listening to test lifecycle events.

```java
public class LoggingExtension implements BeforeEachCallback, AfterEachCallback {
    public void beforeEach(ExtensionContext context) {
        System.out.println("Starting test: " + context.getDisplayName());
    }
    public void afterEach(ExtensionContext context) {
        System.out.println("Finished test: " + context.getDisplayName());
    }
}
```

```java
@ExtendWith(LoggingExtension.class)
class MyTests { … }
```

---

## 6. Running Tests

| Tool | Command |
|------|---------|
| **Maven** | `mvn test` |
| **Gradle** | `gradle test` |
| **IDE** | Right‑click test class → *Run* or *Debug* |
| **JUnit Platform Console Launcher** | `java -jar junit-platform-console-standalone-<ver>.jar --scan-classpath` |

> **CI pipelines**: Add the same build command; the platform will report test results to the console and the CI tool.

---

## 7. Best Practices

| Tip | Why |
|-----|-----|
| **Keep tests small** – test one assertion or behaviour per test. | Easier to locate failures. |
| **Use descriptive names** (`testAdd_WhenAddingTwoNumbers_ShouldReturnSum`). | Self‑documenting. |
| **Avoid flaky tests** – do not rely on randomness or external state. |
| **Arrange‑Act‑Assert** pattern: set up, call, verify. |
| **Mock external dependencies** using `@Mock` / `Mockito`. |
| **Use parameterised tests** to cover multiple scenarios. |
| **Tag tests** (`@Tag("fast")`) and run selective subsets (`-Dgroups=fast`). |
| **Run tests automatically** – configure your IDE or CI to run tests on every build. |
| **Use `assertAll`** to verify a set of related conditions in one go. |
| **Prefer `assertThrowsExactly`** if you need the exact exception type. |
| **Keep test data in a builder or factory** to avoid duplication. |

---

## 8. Quick Reference Cheat‑Sheet

```java
// Imports
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS) // optional
class MyTestClass {

    @BeforeAll void setUpAll() {}
    @BeforeEach void setUp() {}
    @AfterEach void tearDown() {}
    @AfterAll void tearDownAll() {}

    @Test @DisplayName("...")
    void testSomething() {}

    @ParameterizedTest @CsvSource({ "a,b,c", "x,y,z" })
    void paramTest(String a, String b, String c) {}

    @RepeatedTest(5)
    void repeatTest() {}

    @TestFactory
    Stream<DynamicTest> dynamicTests() {
        return Stream.of(
            DynamicTest.dynamicTest("first", () -> assertTrue(true))
        );
    }

    @Nested
    @DisplayName("When performing division")
    class DivisionTests {
        @Test void dividePositive() {}
        @Test void divideByZero() {}
    }

    @Tag("slow")
    @Test void slowTest() {}
}
```

---

### Bottom line

1. **Add the JUnit 5 dependency** to your build.  
2. **Create a test class** in `src/test/java`.  
3. **Annotate methods** with `@Test` (or `@ParameterizedTest`).  
4. **Set up fixtures** with `@BeforeEach/@BeforeAll`.  
5. **Use assertions** (`assertEquals`, `assertThrows`, etc.) to verify behaviour.  
6. **Run** via your IDE or build tool.  

That’s it! Happy testing. 🚀
